# 1. node_modules — the image installs its own inside the container (from
#    package-lock.json), so copying your host's node_modules in would both
#    bloat the build AND risk shipping native-dependency binaries built for
#    your Mac's architecture into a container that might run on a different
#    one (e.g. linux/amd64 in the cloud vs your Mac's arm64).
node_modules

# 2. dist — same idea: the Dockerfile will run `tsc` itself inside the
#    build stage and produce a fresh dist/, so your local one (if it even
#    exists) shouldn't be copied in.
dist

# 3. .env — never bake secrets into an image. Anyone who ever pulls or
#    inspects the image could extract them from its layers, even from a
#    layer that gets "overwritten" later — image layers are historical, not
#    just a final snapshot. Secrets come in at container-RUN time instead
#    (env vars now, a Kubernetes Secret later).
.env

# 4. The Firebase service account JSON — same reasoning as .env, arguably
#    even more sensitive (server/firebase/firebase-service-account.json).
firebase/firebase-service-account.json

# 5. Anything else that's dev-only noise and irrelevant inside a container:
#    git metadata itself (.git), and this repo's own tsconfig.tsbuildinfo
#    (an incremental-build cache file, meaningless in a fresh container).
.git
tsconfig.tsbuildinfo
